WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:03.230
- [Narrator] The price of
virtually every form of energy

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.173
is hitting new lows in 2020.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:07.870
Oil prices are still depressed

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.600
after a historic crash in spring.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.910
Natural gas hit a 25 year low this summer

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:16.273
and prices for coal
continue their descent.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.510
Even the cost of
generating renewable energy

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:20.910
has fallen so sharply

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.433
that it's becoming
competitive with fossil fuels.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.460
The rock bottom energy prices are bad

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.260
for drillers and other producers,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.960
but they're great for consumers

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.050
who have to fill their tanks

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.350
and power their homes

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.150
and there are boosts for businesses

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.950
that consume a lot of energy

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.570
like manufacturers and utilities.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.530
But some experts say this price rout

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.650
across the energy sector
isn't built to last.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.523
Here's how energy got so cheap.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.460
The main thing pushing down prices

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.770
is a huge glut of natural gas.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.680
Production sky-rocketed in the U.S.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.360
over the last decade and a half

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.250
as shale drillers unearthed
troves of the fuel.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:01.040
- We started to build U.S. gas supply

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.090
just ahead of the Great Recession,

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.100
anticipating a real exponential growth

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.810
in energy demand globally.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.700
We built capacity toward a demand peak

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.503
that never materialized.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.390
- [Narrator] The rise in
production swamped the market,

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.530
pushing down prices, even as the U.S.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.150
burned more natural gas than ever in 2019.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.240
Then the coronavirus shut down businesses

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.800
around the globe.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.310
As Americans hunkered down,

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.760
demand for electricity plunged.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.040
Economists estimate that roughly half

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.100
of the American workforce
is now working from home.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.960
With workers gone, office
buildings and factories

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.380
turned off the lights
and the air conditioning.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:46.300
- Back in April, when
COVID containment measures

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.650
were at their peak, overall
power demand was down 10%.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.990
- [Narrator] As demand
sunk, U.S. gas producers

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.400
were slow to respond and dial back output

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.670
which added to the glut,

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.480
driving down wholesale
prices even further.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.340
Cheap natural gas which is the
biggest source of electricity

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.500
in the United States has
meant lower power prices.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.410
Here's a look at prices in 2020,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.613
versus past years.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.200
In New York City, prices
were down by about half,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.003
compared to the five year average.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.530
As gas prices bottomed out,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.380
a similar dynamic was playing out in oil.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.360
This spring, crude prices
took an unprecedented turn

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.140
with futures contracts
trading in negative territory

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.990
for the first time ever.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.080
- We think of it more, not
so much as a perfect storm,

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.250
but rather, death by a thousand cuts

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.360
for these oil producers.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.943
Over the years, U.S. production grew.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.360
To some extent, global
demand didn't quite reach

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.520
the expectations on which producers

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.920
hinged their production plans

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.860
and we ended up with
more oil in the market

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.880
than we knew what to do with

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.600
and then COVID was the last shoe to drop

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.030
and cut things in a way

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.500
that we've certainly never
seen before in the industry.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.840
We simply ran out of space to put the oil.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.230
- [Narrator] Within a few
weeks, prices bounced back,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.610
but are still low enough
to bankrupt oil companies.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.590
This pushed down gasoline
prices across the country.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.803
In some regions, prices
dropped below $1 a gallon.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.320
Though fewer people
are driving and flying,

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.130
those who are, are reaping the benefits.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.980
Here's the retail price of
gasoline in the United States.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.610
You can see, at its lowest point,

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:33.610
the average price was $1.77,
down from $2.78 in July 2019.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.760
The cost to install new,
renewable energy projects

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.580
is plunging, too.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.330
The long-held theory is
that low fossil fuel prices

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.450
would hamper the development
of renewable energy projects,

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.300
but that's not how it's playing out.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.430
Renewable energy has gotten cheaper

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:51.543
presenting power buyers with options.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.820
- Certainly in the United
States, previously a lot of

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:59.610
renewable energy deployment
was driven by state mandates.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.135
Now because the price of
energy for these technologies

00:04:03.135 --> 00:04:07.240
has dropped so rapidly, many
utilities and energy purchasers

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.210
are choosing to buy wind and solar

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.330
for clearly economic reasons.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:13.940
- [Narrator] This chart
shows the levelized price

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:15.170
of different types of energy

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:16.990
which accounts for production costs.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.430
- The levelized cost of
energy is trying to capture

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.430
the total lifetime costs
of an energy asset,

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.657
so it captures upfront
costs, operating costs,

00:04:29.657 --> 00:04:33.070
and benefits associated with that system.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:34.560
- [Narrator] You can see that prices

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.010
for new, renewable projects

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.800
have fallen over the last decade.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.670
In some cases, sharply.

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:42.270
That's in part, thanks to a
decade of low interest rates

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.170
which helped fuel the shale drilling boom

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.630
and made renewable energy
projects cheaper, too.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:49.667
But it's also do to
advances in technology.

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.737
- [David] Scaling up that supply chain

00:04:51.737 --> 00:04:53.327
has really reduced costs.

00:04:53.327 --> 00:04:56.100
- [Narrator] Declining costs
to produce renewable energy

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.370
have resulted in growing
supplies of green power

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.270
around the world, pushing down prices,

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.510
and encouraging consumption.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:05.320
In the U.S., more electricity
was consumed last year

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.520
with renewable sources than with coal

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.203
for the first time in more than 130 years.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.000
So what do these trends
mean for consumers?

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.940
The lower prices aren't always evident

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.700
on your monthly bill, due
to taxes and other factors,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:21.820
but the real cost of electricity
to consumers and businesses

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.100
has declined slightly
over the last decade.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.900
- Analysts expect, at the end
of the day, in the long term

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.400
for electricity prices to go down.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.490
It's already based off of
technology improvements,

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:35.690
the lowering costs of wind and solar,

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.353
but some of it is also regulatory.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:40.910
- [Narrator] In the short term however,

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.270
experts say prices could rise.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:44.570
Producers turned off the spigots

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:46.380
in hopes of reducing the glut

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.830
and now, demand for natural
gas is picking back up.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.690
- Those two trends don't work together.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.560
Growing demand on one side,
encouraged by the low prices

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.610
and declining supply with
producers who simply can't

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:00.210
make things work with the
gas prices where they are.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.550
Do we think, by the
winter, you're gonna see

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:06.670
a much stronger gas price come through?

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.700
Do we think we're at a
point where gas prices

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.780
are unsustainably low, such
that they have to rise?

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.470
- [Narrator] What is certain is that 2020

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:16.360
is shaping up to be a historic year

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.657
of abundant, cheaply produced energy.

